uitest-sdk — third-party binaries bundled with this package
===========================================================

This file records what ships in `assets/` beyond this repository's own
source code. It is a factual inventory, not a licence grant.

1. What the MIT licence covers
------------------------------

`LICENSE` (MIT) covers the source code of this repository: everything under
`src/`, `scripts/`, `test/`, `site/`, `examples/` and the prose under `docs/`.

It does not cover the binaries listed in section 2. They are not built from
this repository's source.

2. The binaries
---------------

All three are device-side extensions produced and signed by Huawei
Technologies as part of DevEco Studio / DevEco Testing. They are redistributed
here unmodified so that a caller can run the SDK against a device without
first locating a DevEco installation. The SDK loads them over `hdc`; it does
not link against them.

| ABI | File | Version | Bytes | MD5 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| arm64 | `agent.so` | 1.1.10 | 600246 | `ebd787f91a55c968d837f09e80ad10cd` |
| arm64 | `libscreen_casting.z.so` | 6.8-20260801 | 247989 | `8c8df9a96af21aac71d5091ffa0b1477` |
| x86_64 | `agent.so` | 1.1.9 | 1460181 | `c094217d5ab7f8eaefebbb90307c3a3b` |

Where each was obtained (see `assets/manifest.json` for the machine-readable
form):

- arm64 `agent.so` — `DevEco Testing/python/Lib/site-packages/devicetest/res/prototype/native/uitest_agent_v1.1.10.so`
- arm64 `libscreen_casting.z.so` — from the hscrcpy server (HOScrcpy 2.12.9 build)
- x86_64 `agent.so` — `@deveco/deveco-cli/node_modules/@deveco/deveco-cli-common/src/resources/uitest/uitest_agent_v1.1.9.x86_64.so`

The casting binary's provenance is examined at length in
`docs/09-capability-gaps-roadmap.md` §1.6. The bundled 6.8 build was committed
to the hscrcpy repository as "add HOScrcpy 2.12.9 server SO 6.8/6.9-20260801";
the 7.0 build discussed there is a different file and is not bundled here. The
repository (hscrcpy) is MIT; that licence covers the repository's source, not
the signed binary inside it.

3. Status of the binaries
-------------------------

No licence, express or implied, is granted for the binaries in section 2.
They remain the property of Huawei Technologies and are subject to whatever
terms accompany DevEco Studio and DevEco Testing. They are included solely
for interoperability: without them the SDK has nothing to talk to.

Because the binaries are signed (the device's XPM check rejects unsigned
`.so` files — see `docs/06-signing-xpm.md`), they cannot be rebuilt or
modified. If you would rather not redistribute them, delete the three files
under `assets/` and pass your own copies via `Device.connect({ extensionDir })`.

